Metrics|Metrics are numeric measurements aggregated over time with identifying labels or dimensions.|They efficiently show trends and support alerts, but aggregation discards individual event detail. Choose units, temporality and label cardinality deliberately. A metric name without a definition and population is easy to misinterpret.|
Logs|Logs record discrete events with contextual fields and timestamps.|Structured fields make filtering and correlation more reliable than parsing inconsistent text. Include useful identifiers while excluding credentials and unnecessary personal data. Sampling, buffering and retention determine which events remain available.|
Traces|Distributed traces connect spans describing work across a request's path.|Parent-child and causal links reveal where time is spent and which dependencies participate. Instrumentation and sampling may omit parts of the path. A trace is evidence of one observed execution, not a complete population-level latency distribution.|
Counters|A counter accumulates occurrences or quantities and normally increases until it resets.|Use a reset-aware rate or increase calculation for interval behavior. Summing raw counters from restarting instances can produce misleading results. Define exactly which event increments the counter and avoid double counting retries as unique users.|
Gauges|A gauge reports a value that can rise or fall, such as queue depth or current memory usage.|It represents a sampled state rather than an accumulated event count. A rate calculation intended for counters may not be meaningful on a gauge. Sampling frequency can miss short peaks, so choose collection intervals around the use case.|
Histograms|Histograms record a distribution using buckets and count or sum information.|Bucket boundaries determine resolution near important thresholds. Classic Prometheus histogram buckets are cumulative and support aggregation before estimating quantiles. The estimate is limited by bucket design; it is not an exact percentile of retained raw events.|
Labels|Labels distinguish metric time series by dimensions such as service, route or status.|Every distinct label combination creates another series. Unbounded request IDs or user IDs can overwhelm storage and query capacity. Use bounded dimensions for metrics and put high-cardinality event detail in appropriately controlled logs or traces.|
Dashboards|Dashboards organize signals to answer operational questions.|Start with user outcomes, then expose dependency and resource diagnostics. Show units, time windows and missing-data behavior. A visually green dashboard is not meaningful if it omits the failing user path or averages away a regional outage.|
Rate before aggregation|Reset-aware rate calculation should be applied to individual counter series before they are aggregated.|Otherwise one instance's reset can be hidden by another instance's increasing value. In PromQL, sum(rate(counter[window])) preserves per-series reset handling. Choose a window long enough to contain useful samples.|
Percentiles|A percentile describes a value below which a specified share of observations falls.|The p99 is not a maximum and cannot generally be combined by averaging instance p99 values. Aggregate suitable distributions first, then estimate the percentile. Sparse samples and different populations can make tail estimates unstable.|
Missing data|Missing telemetry means a measurement was not observed, which is different from observing zero.|Collection failure, discovery changes, label changes or an idle system can cause gaps. Define alert behavior explicitly and monitor the telemetry pipeline itself. Treating absent errors as zero errors can falsely declare an unobserved service healthy.|
Scrape health|Scrape health describes whether a metrics collector can discover and retrieve target data.|A successful scrape indicates collection worked, not that the application is healthy. Inspect target errors, duration and sample volume. A target can disappear from discovery entirely, requiring separate expected-target checks.|
Recording rules|Recording rules precompute query results into new time series.|They reduce repeated query cost and standardize shared calculations. Evaluation interval and dependency order influence freshness. Version and test rule changes because a wrong recording rule can spread incorrect results across many dashboards and alerts.|
Alert duration|An alert duration requires a condition to remain true for a configured period before firing.|It reduces noise from short fluctuations but delays detection. Choose it from the tolerated impact and signal behavior, not as a blanket way to hide unstable alerts. Pending and firing states should be understood during tests.|
Alert grouping|Alert grouping combines related alerts into fewer notifications.|Grouping keys should preserve the information needed to assign ownership and act. Overly broad grouping can hide independent incidents; overly narrow grouping creates floods. Grouping changes notification presentation, not the underlying alert condition.|
Alert inhibition|Inhibition suppresses notifications for selected alerts when a related source alert is active.|For example, a cluster outage can suppress many dependent service symptoms. Matching labels must prevent unrelated alerts from being silenced. Test both suppression and release after recovery so important signals do not remain hidden.|
Sampling|Sampling retains a subset of telemetry to control cost and processing overhead.|Head sampling decides near the start of a trace and is inexpensive but may miss rare failures. The sampled population can be biased. Do not infer exact request counts from sampled traces without understanding the sampling method.|
Tail sampling|Tail sampling waits for enough trace information to choose traces based on completed behavior.|It can favor errors or slow requests but requires buffering and trace-consistent routing. Late spans, memory pressure and incomplete traces affect decisions. Size the collector and define behavior when sampling capacity is exceeded.|
Context propagation|Context propagation carries trace and related identifiers across process boundaries.|HTTP headers or message metadata let downstream spans join the same trace. Instrument both sender and receiver and preserve context through asynchronous work. Trust boundaries must prevent arbitrary inbound context from becoming an authorization signal.|
Correlation|Correlation connects signals using shared attributes such as trace ID, service identity and time.|A log linked to a trace can explain a slow span while metrics show prevalence. Correlated timing alone does not establish causation. Check whether populations, clocks and deployment versions align before drawing conclusions.|
Sensitive telemetry|Telemetry can contain credentials, personal information and business-sensitive payloads.|Collect only useful fields, redact at the source where possible and restrict access and retention. A later dashboard mask does not remove data already stored. Review exception messages, URLs and high-cardinality attributes for accidental disclosure.|
Retention|Retention defines how long telemetry is kept and at what resolution.|Longer retention supports historical investigations but costs storage and increases exposure. Downsampling preserves some trends while losing detail. Align retention with incident response needs and ensure deletion policies cover backups and derived datasets where required.|
Telemetry pipelines|A telemetry pipeline receives, processes and exports signals between applications and backends.|Queues, batching, retries and memory limits affect reliability under backpressure. A blocked exporter can exhaust collector memory or drop data. Monitor queue age, refused data and export failures independently of application metrics.|
Resource identity|Resource attributes identify the entity producing telemetry, such as service, environment and instance.|Consistent identity enables correct grouping across metrics, logs and traces. A changing or missing service name can fragment a dashboard. Avoid using unstable instance identifiers where a stable service-level aggregation is intended.|
Clock skew|Clock skew is disagreement between clocks used to timestamp events.|It can make cross-host logs appear out of order and distort apparent span timing. Synchronize wall clocks and use causal IDs and monotonic durations where appropriate. Do not infer a negative processing duration is an application behavior without checking clocks.|
RED signals|RED tracks request rate, errors and duration for request-driven services.|It starts diagnosis from demand, success and user-perceived speed. Define what counts as a request and error for the service. Pair RED with resource and dependency signals when investigating the cause.|
USE signals|USE examines utilization, saturation and errors for each resource.|CPU busy time, runnable queues and hardware errors answer different questions. High utilization without saturation may be acceptable; a queue can reveal a bottleneck before average utilization looks extreme. Inventory relevant resources rather than checking CPU alone.|
Synthetic checks|Synthetic checks execute controlled probes of selected user paths.|They can detect failures before organic traffic arrives and test outside-in availability. Probe location, credentials and test data determine coverage. A simple health endpoint cannot represent a full checkout or login transaction.|
Instrumentation cost|Instrumentation consumes CPU, memory, network and backend capacity.|High event volume, expensive labels and synchronous export can affect the application being measured. Bound overhead, batch exports and test under load. More telemetry is useful only when it improves decisions at an acceptable cost.|
Evidence and causality|Observability evidence supports hypotheses about why a system behaves as it does.|Compare affected and unaffected cohorts, align changes with the timeline and test predictions. A graph moving at the same time as an outage is a clue, not proof. Record uncertainty and seek evidence that could disprove the leading hypothesis.|
